1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. g3 Bd7 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. O-O
Bg7 9. Nde2 Qc8 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. exd5 Ne5 12. Be3 O-O 13. Bd4 Bh3 14. b3 Bxg2
15. Kxg2 Qf5 16. f3 Rfc8 17. c3 h5 18. Qb1 Qd7 19. h3 Qb5 20. c4 Qb4 21. Bc3
Qc5 22. Bd4 Qb4 23. f4 Qd2 24. fxe5 dxe5 25. Qb2 Qd3 26. Rf3 Qe4 27. Be3 $2 ({
A doubt about the score creeps in here. The score gives 27.B-K3, and the
columnist giving the game backs it up, but was it perhaps a scoring error?
Hard to believe both Alexander and Aitken missed this fairly simple tactic.
However, had the more plausible 27.Bc3 (B-B3) been played, then White's 28th
move would have left the knight en prise on e2.} 27. Bc3 {makes a lot more
sense and would avert the trick that Black missed. But now} g5 {and} 28. Qb1 $4
{is an unfeasible blunder.}) 27... g5 (27... Qxf3+ $1 28. Kxf3 e4+ {with a
likely Black win. Dugald MacIsaac, in his Glasgow Herald column of 12 Nov 1948,
sticks to his original version of the score when he points out the above trick,
saying "this surprising error, spotted by several of our readers, seems to
have been generally overlooked."}) 28. Qb1 {Source: Glasgow Herald, 22 Oct
1948 (a few moves of the game given in BCM, Oct 1948, p345)} 1-0

