1. e4 e6 2. g3 ({A pleasant change from the routine move} 2. d4) 2... d5 3. Bg2
dxe4 4. Nc3 Nf6 ({It would be exceedingly dangerous to win a pawn by} 4... f5
5. f3 exf3 6. Nxf3) 5. Nxe4 Nxe4 6. Bxe4 Nd7 7. d4 Nf6 8. Bg2 Be7 9. Ne2 O-O
10. O-O {[White retains an advantage because of his well-placed g2 B, Black's
next moves aim at isolating White's d-pawn, or alternatively at freeing his
own c8 B.]} c5 11. c3 cxd4 12. Nxd4 e5 13. Nb3 Qc7 14. Qe2 Be6 15. Re1 Nd7 16.
f4 Bd6 17. fxe5 Nxe5 18. Bf4 Nc4 19. Bxd6 Nxd6 20. Nd4 Bc4 21. Qf2 Rae8 22. b3
Bd3 23. c4 Qc5 24. Rad1 Be4 25. Nf5 Qxf5 26. Rxd6 Qxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Bxg2 28. Rxe8
Rxe8 29. Kxg2 Kf8 30. Kf3 Ke7 31. Rd4 Rc8 32. Ke4 Rc6 33. c5 Rf6 34. Rd2 Rf1
35. Kd5 Rc1 36. b4 Rb1 37. Kc4 a6 38. a3 Rc1+ 39. Kb3 a5 40. Ka4 (40. Rd6 {
would probably have won - BCM/Wade}) 40... axb4 41. axb4 Ra1+ 42. Kb3 Ra8 43.
Kc4 h5 44. b5 h4 45. c6 bxc6 46. b6 hxg3 47. hxg3 Ke6 48. Kc5 Ra1 49. b7 Rb1
50. Kxc6 Rc1+ 51. Kb6 Rb1+ 52. Kc7 Rc1+ 53. Kd8 Rb1 54. Rd7 g5 55. Re7+ Kf5 56.
Rxf7+ Kg4 57. Rg7 Kf3 58. Kc8 g4 59. b8=Q Rxb8+ 60. Kxb8 Kxg3 61. Kc7 Kf3 {
Source: BCM, Jan 1949, p30. Curiously, the BCM of Nov 1948, p377, gives the
length of this game as 52, but the game as given in the Jan 1949 issue was
annotated by Broadbent so can be assumed to be correct.} 1/2-1/2

